WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.674
A tool is a small capability with a name, input contract, output

00:00:04.674 --> 00:00:06.232
contract, and defined effect.

00:00:06.382 --> 00:00:10.902
Narrow tools are easier to authorize and test than a generic database

00:00:10.902 --> 00:00:12.786
query or unrestricted HTTP fetch.

00:00:12.936 --> 00:00:16.399
Separate read operations from actions that alter business state.

00:00:16.549 --> 00:00:21.316
Tool descriptions help the model select a capability; Java code must enforce

00:00:21.316 --> 00:00:22.110
its constraints.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:28.421
Derive tenant, user, permissions, and correlation identifiers from trusted request context.

00:00:28.571 --> 00:00:31.446
Never let the model choose which account it represents.

00:00:31.596 --> 00:00:35.376
Apply authorization at the data access boundary as well as at the

00:00:35.376 --> 00:00:35.691
UI.

00:00:35.841 --> 00:00:40.208
Keep tool output small and include a stable source identifier so later

00:00:40.208 --> 00:00:42.028
explanations can point to evidence.

00:00:42.178 --> 00:00:47.122
lookupOrder receives orderId, finds the synthetic order, and checks both tenant and

00:00:47.122 --> 00:00:47.535
owner.

00:00:47.685 --> 00:00:52.335
An unknown order and an inaccessible order return the same public message,

00:00:52.335 --> 00:00:53.498
limiting account enumeration.

00:00:53.648 --> 00:00:59.101
searchPolicy is deliberately a fixed policy fixture; the retrieval module later teaches

00:00:59.101 --> 00:01:00.919
a searchable document collection.

00:01:01.069 --> 00:01:03.688
Neither fixture is a live commerce integration.

00:01:03.838 --> 00:01:05.739
Design a delivery-estimate tool.

00:01:05.889 --> 00:01:11.654
Specify allowed inputs, the trusted identity source, maximum response size, timeout, and

00:01:11.654 --> 00:01:15.016
behavior when the delivery service is unavailable.

00:01:15.166 --> 00:01:19.854
Accept an order identifier, then resolve the authorized shipment server-side.

00:01:20.004 --> 00:01:24.586
Return an estimate with timestamp and source, or a typed unavailable result.

00:01:24.736 --> 00:01:27.623
Reject arbitrary URLs and customer IDs.

00:01:27.773 --> 00:01:31.612
Use a finite deadline and do not replace missing delivery data with

00:01:31.612 --> 00:01:32.572
an invented date.

00:01:32.722 --> 00:01:33.393
Question.

00:01:33.543 --> 00:01:35.613
Why avoid a generic SQL tool?

00:01:35.763 --> 00:01:36.339
Answer.

00:01:36.489 --> 00:01:40.899
Its authority is broad, schema exposure can be large, and safely constraining

00:01:40.899 --> 00:01:43.104
queries and effects is much harder.

00:01:43.254 --> 00:01:43.925
Question.

00:01:44.075 --> 00:01:45.970
Where should tenant filtering occur?

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:46.696
Answer.

00:01:46.846 --> 00:01:51.466
At the trusted query or service boundary before data reaches the model,

00:01:51.466 --> 00:01:53.391
with downstream authorization still enforced.
